<content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Jesse’s car gets 30 miles per gallon of gas.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write an equation using direct variation for distance, \(d\), and number of gallons of gas, \(g\) for this problem.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Use the equation for direct variation \( y=kx \). So, \( d=kg \).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>If Jesse travels 150 miles he uses 5 gallons of gas. Find the constant of variation.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Use the equation for direct variation \( y=kx \). So, \(150=k(5)\). The constant of variation is \( k=30 \).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Now, write the equation if Jesse travels \(d\) miles with a car that uses 30 miles per gallon of gas.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( d=30g \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>If Las Vegas is 285 miles away, how many gallons of gas are needed to get there?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Substitute 285 miles in for \( d \) in the equation. So, \( 285=30g \) and \( g=9.5 \).</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para>
</section>
</content>
